myanmar's ousted leader aung san soo chi  is back in court today facing a new  trial for alleged corruption  the pro-democracy leader has faced a  long list of charges since her  government was overthrown by a military  coup in february the junta has ramped up  violence to try and crush a popular  uprising the u.n is warning that the  window of opportunity to stop the army  from entrenching his rule is growing  smaller by the day  the people protest  the military cracks down  it's a cycle that's been repeated many  times in myanmar's history  most recently earlier this year the  military deposed the country's fledgling  democratic government in february and  arrested its leaders including aung san  tsuchi  since then she's been under house arrest  facing a wide array of charges including  incitement the deliberate spreading of  misinformation to agitate the people  violating covet 19 restrictions  and illegally importing walkie-talkies  to be used by her supporters  if found guilty the 76 year old could  face decades in prison  meanwhile the human rights situation in  myanmar has deteriorated rapidly since  the coup  over 1  100 individuals have reportedly now died  at the hands of the security forces  since the coup  over 8 000 individuals including  children have been arrested since the  coup with over 4 700 remaining in  detention  most are held without any form of due  process and lack access to legal counsel  or even the ability to communicate with  their families  the military government faces increasing  international isolation  and a cratering economy that's seen  widespread shortages with a humanitarian  crisis looming  the un has called for sushi's immediate  release as a first step towards  restoring order  yet her trial is set to go ahead  as resistance in the streets continues  despite the government's harshest  efforts to suppress it  for more now i'm joined by pulitzer  prize-winning journalist amen tun who's  currently based in bangtan bangkok good  to see you again first of all those  charges against aung sang su chi her  supporters say they're trumped up that  the case is politically motivated how do  you see it  so what we're seeing from the hunter now  is the persecution of top political  leadership in myanmar  under a host of charges the most recent  space of charges has been  for corruption we've seen various  ministers and  local government leaders charged with  corruption with very little evidence to  show  how they could have committed these  crimes or that these crimes indeed took  place  i think in the case of outside sushi we  also need to look at some of the other  things that she has been charged with  one of which is incitement of public  unrest which uh with the hunters citing  the public's response to the coup as  evidence of her crime and yet with her  we see that she was arrested on the very  first day of the coup in the pre-dawn  air in the pre-dawn of hours and so it's  very difficult to see how she could have  committed at least this crime which uh  obviously brings uh into question the  legitimacy of many of these other  charges that she is being  held  without  very little access to our lawyers  and in an unknown location for  following the full military takeover  there were massive protests that were  violently crushed what has become of  that protest movement  yeah so in the first few weeks of the  protest we saw hundreds of thousands if  not millions of people in the streets  and we saw that you know people really  faced the the high risk of death  for conducting those protests so what  we're seeing now is a  nationwide protests are still ongoing  however they take the form of these  small flash strikes that are happening  in various cities and towns across the  country  they're largely organized to be less  than you know 10 or 20 minutes  and they are sort of very ad hoc and  involve very few people in order for  people to be able to continue to show  the world that they do not consent to  this form of government um and yet are  trying to stay safe we are also seeing a  continuation of the civil disobedience  movement which has seen over a hundred  thousand civil servants who are  continuing to strike and continuing to  refuse to work for the hunter many of  whom are now in hiding as the junta has  been trying to sort of hunt down these  striking workers people are continuing  to boycott military-owned and affiliated  companies and we've also seen a civil  government that has risen up and  declared themselves to be the legitimate  government of myanmar but we are  definitely seeing a decrease in uh  in-street protest as  these forms of resistance become more  and more dangerous  now the u.n secretary general this week  gave a stark warning about the myanmar  crisis he said there's a risk of  large-scale armed conflict and what he  called a multi-dimensional catastrophe  what is he do you have any idea what  he's referring to there  yeah so myanmar is technically at war  at the moment the the civilian  government i was just talking about the  national unity government has declared  what they're calling a people's  defensive war uh  calling on the people of the country to  fight against the military so what we're  seeing now is more and more armed  clashes taking place  between uh local non-military militias  uh against the military we're seeing a  number of targeted assassinations as  well as the use of bombs and land mines  and  really we're seeing a stalemate in which  the military is unable to fully repress  uh the people and the armed insurrection  that is taking place across the country  right now and these local militias are  largely unable and do not have the  firepower to actually defeat the  military  and you know on top of the fact that  there is now essentially a war that's  taking place across the country myanmar  was already a fairly poor country to  begin with with weak healthcare  infrastructure as well as education  infrastructure and there's a real risk  that millions of people could fall into  food insecurity in the coming months  and economists are predicting a  double-digit contraction in the economy  in the next year  journalist ayman tan there in bangkok  thank you very much  thank you for having me  you
 

